    "result" : {
      "candidates" : [ {
        "abstract" : "This paper examines whether Iran can lawfully restrict navigation in the Strait of Hormuz under international law. While the regime of transit passage under the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea (UNCLOS) guarantees continuous and unobstructed navigation through international straits, its applicability to Iran remains contested due to its non-ratification of the Convention. The analysis therefore considers whether transit passage reflects customary international law and engages with Iran’s reliance on sovereignty and the persistent objector doctrine. It also explores the fallback regime of innocent passage and its limits. The paper argues that, although international law does not readily permit the closure of the Strait of Hormuz, the governing legal framework remains uncertain and contingent upon State acceptance and practice.",
